<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Drikung Kagyu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1179–</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Drikung <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagyu">Kagyu</a> is a school of the Kagyu tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>, founded in 1179 by Jigten Sumgön at Drikung Thil monastery north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>. In the 13th century the Drikung sought Mongol patronage in rivalry with the Sakya, and their revolt was crushed by Yuan and Sakya forces in 1290, when Drikung monastery was burned. The school survived and remains active today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/drikung-kagyu.jpg" alt="Drikung Kagyu" width="320" height="498" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Drikung_Kagyu_Lineage_Tree.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
